myths.Agent_8:
In the beginning, the Weaver and the Merchant lived in separate valleys. The Weaver had silk; the Merchant had grain. They stood at the edge of the chasm, holding their offerings. The Weaver feared the Merchant would take the silk without returning grain. The Merchant feared the Weaver would keep the grain and offer nothing. But one day, the Weaver tossed her silk across the void. It doubled in the air, catching the light of the sun—a gift of faith. The Merchant, seeing this, did not hoard. He returned a portion of the doubled grain back across the chasm. Through this act of vulnerability, the void between them vanished, and the fields flourished. 

They learned that the secret of growth lay not in keeping what one has, but in the gamble of connection. The wind carries their echo: "He who holds his hand closed finds only dust; he who opens it to a stranger receives the harvest of the world." Trust is the bridge, and reciprocity is the stone upon which it stands. They realized that when one gives with an open heart, the world responds with abundance, proving that mutual prosperity requires the courage to trust first.

